# A logpoint whose parts are numbers runs in the program: setting it says
# so, and each hit writes its values to a ring the debugger reads, so a
# million logged hits take the program under five seconds (it times them
# itself) and every message is still shown, in the order of the hits. A
# stop per hit costs tens of microseconds, so a million stops would take
# twenty seconds at the least; the hits themselves take about a tenth of a
# second on an idle machine, and the bound leaves room for a loaded one.
# A logpoint with a String part says, when it is set, that it can't run in
# the program; the debugger fills in its message at each hit.
# The fixture must stay in jet fmt form: line 10 is the `total += step` of
# count_up's loop and line 19 that of named's. The count comes from the
# program's arguments (none here, so a million): with a literal count the
# compiler works count_up's total out ahead and its loop never runs.
fixture: PlacedLogpointRunsInTheProgram.fixture
env: NO_COLOR=1
env: PATH={work}/no-tools
step: jet debug prog.jet
stdin: placed_logpoint_runs_in_the_program.stdin
exit: 0
stdout: ~ "\nThe logpoint runs in the program: a hit writes its values there and runs on without a stop\n" # the logpoint runs in the program
stdout: ~ "\nThe logpoint can't run in the program: `word` is a String, which the debugger would read after the hit rather than at it; the debugger fills in its message at each hit, which stops the program each time\n" # a String part keeps it out of the program, and says why
stdout: ~ "a million logged hits under five seconds\n" # a million logged hits run on without a stop each
stdout: ~ "\ns0\ns1\ns2\ns3\ns4\ns5\ns6\ns0\n" # each hit's message, in the order of the hits
stdout: ~ "even at 0\nodd at 1\n" # the debugger fills in the other logpoint at each hit
stdout: ~ "totals 2999997 7\n" # the program runs to its totals unchanged
stdout: ~ "Program finished\n" # and to its end
